  Selected characters from 16th century fonts are discussed in
  the paper, notably Kaspar Hochfeder's font no.~2. In particular
  their representation in the Unicode
  standard is investigated. For some of them the encoding is obvious,
  but there are also characters for which their encoding remains an
  open question. Some old characters don't have Unicode codepoints,
  but can be printed with an appropriate OpenType/TrueType fonts using
  typographic features. Two such fonts are used: Junicode and
  Incunable. Some characters from Gutenberg's bible are also
  mentioned.
